live with a Bible in one hand at a  newspaper in the other this is the  advice attributed to theologian Karl  Bart they do not have equal weight for  Bart unless of course you have the full  Sunday Times the Bible is weightier Bart  would suggest to interpret the newspaper  through the lens of the Bible but  nonetheless live your life with the  Bible in one hand in the newspaper in  the other and so this morning with a  Bible in one hand we are given two  parables the parable of the lost sheep  the story of that one sheep who  rebellious Lee wanders off and the Good  Shepherd who seeks after that sheep and  brings it back home and the parable of  the lost coin the woman scours her  entire house weeping every corner and  finally finds it with the newspaper in  the other hand we read about floods and  fire child soldiers and investment  bankers Fashion Week and unemployment we  read that it has been a very rough week  for our Muslim neighbors with someone  threatening to burn the quran and the  end of the holy month of ramadan and the  anniversary of September 11 2001 all  overlapping with what is usually a  joyous celebration this weekend of Eid  outfitter we read in these pages and we  see in the lives of our neighbors that  we are on tender ground this week also  marked the Jewish holy days of Rosh  Hashanah the Jewish New Year a time to  mark a new beginning but also a time to  look back at what has been especially in  these liminal days now leading up to yom  kippur a time for atonement the time for  repentance and so are Jewish and Muslim  neighbors are in a season of  contemplation diligent prayer repentance  and I'm afraid that we or at least I am  often not so good  that yet with these parables in one hand  and the newspaper in the other the need  for Christians to take time for  examination and repentance seems very  clear you see when I hear the parable of  the lost sheep I am pretty sure the lost  sheep is somebody else someone I know  who is wandered far away from God  someone whose behavior I dislike someone  may be like that pastor in florida  threatening to burn the quran but then I  stop and think I don't want him in my  flock he can't be the one Jesus would be  seeking maybe someone like him but less  evil someone who just wanders away but  not me I'm not the lost sheep if I were  Sharon might fire me my bishop would be  really upset my congregation would stop  listening yet this parable defines the  lost sheep as a sinner and I am  certainly not exempt from sin if the 99  sheep represent those in need of no  repentance well if I'm honest I'm a lost  sheep and aren't we all Martin Luther  was big on paradox and one of the most  essential for him was the dual reality  for each of us as saint and sinner we  are always partially with the 99 sheep  with our lives in order living in  harmony making the Angels proud and we  are always partially that one sheep who  has wandered into the wilderness the  part of ourselves that Jesus is still  seeking to transform this is the paradox  we can take with us into reading the  newspaper or wandering the streets of  New Haven many times this week I was  tempted to yell at the TV when threats  of violence or the sensational ization  of opposing opinions just really got on  my nerves but the parable of the lost  sheep asks us to think carefully about  our own assumptions the walls and our  heads and hearts the ignorance that not  only lives in a lone voice crying out  for violence but also deep within my own  bones it is tempting to distance  ourselves from what others do in the  name of Jesus we do not want to be  lumped together with the very few  radical Christians perpetuating hate but  let's be honest about our own  shortcomings to our their small ways we  have let such such acts happen through  our stunned silence do we ever assume  the radical acts of a few represent the  whole group the parable of the lost  sheep calls for deep humility because  the second we assume we are safe in the  99 with our lives completely in order we  have darted back out into the wilderness  of pride yet again the second we think  we are the good shepherd with the task  of seeking the lost we must also  remember we are a sheep draped over the  shoulders of the shepherd who sought us  out and is carrying us home even now as  a freshman in college I was part of a  wonderful christian fellowship group we  met weekly for singing in Bible study we  became fast friends was a tight group  this group saved me when my roommate was  driving me crazy when basketball was  exhausting me but I remember a  particular moment when one of my  basketball teammates also a Christian  said she could never come to that group  she assumed there would be no room for  her everyone already knew each other so  well they all spoke the same language  she had a lot of questions about God and  assumed they would not be welcome in  that group whether or not these things  were actually true she had experienced  them to be true and she really got me  thinking at I know kind of tight knit  community is what we're all seeking in a  church or a Bible study or a fellowship  or prayer group these are our homes away  from home  our ports in the storm they become our  families but the flip side of being part  of that tight knit group meant that I  had helped my teammate get the  impression that she was the one and we  were the 99 there wasn't room for her  that we were the saints and she was the  sinner I ended up transitioning to into  other campus ministries during my time  in college but in retrospect I actually  regret not talking to that group's  leaders a little bit I regret not asking  some hard questions I regret not looking  around and asking is everyone here or is  anyone left out if one feels excluded we  are not whole we cannot be all things to  all people but are we really reflecting  God's open welcome of course we don't  have that completely figured out here at  the university church either you'll see  a long statement of welcome in your blue  bulletin inserts but we can never fully  reflect the extravagance of God's  welcome we'll just keep trying but  sometimes we assume you're all familiar  with liturgical language or even know  what the word liturgical means sometimes  we assume that everyone can read music  as well as the choir and yet being asked  to sing in public might be the strangest  thing you've been ever asked to do this  is a time to remember that it is God's  welcome that it is Christ persistent  searching for us until the circle is  complete that we are trying to reflect  we do not create it we just try to  reflect it welcoming one another is an  ongoing task one way we try to reflect  this is by inviting everyone to take  part in this church however they like as  we recognized leaders and commissioned  deacons this morning remember these are  not exclusive groups of chosen ones but  instead they recommend they represent  the larger whole of our congregation  because every single one of you here  today even if this is your first time he  you are an important an integral part of  this community in another way we try to  reflect God's welcome is by trying to be  good neighbors to our Jewish and Muslim  and Buddhist and atheist friends as well  as fellow Christians with whom we might  have some significant disagreements and  nonetheless they are our neighbors in  the parable of the lost coin the woman  has 10 coins and loses just one but if  we lost ten percent of our treasures we  would work really hard to recover them  if ten percent of our neighbors are  being persecuted it's time to speak up  if ten percent of more more of the  world's population is starving we to  hunger for justice at the heart of both  of these parables we remember there is  inherent value in every part of the  whole and so the woman picks up her  broom and cleans every inch of her  humble home looking desperately for that  lost coin and then the coin is found  what was lost is now returned again the  neighbor facing hostility knows she has  an ally the student questioning knows he  has a place here God is like the woman  sweeping dust out of the corners of our  lives making us new gathering in what  was lost we are the coin in God's hand a  treasured life yet there are other coins  as part of this collective treasure  coins made up of our neighbors even  those with whom we disagree those who  help us remember who we are being in a  diverse multi-faith community might be a  new thing for many of you it was for me  at various points in my life but at  least for me it has helped to strengthen  my Christian faith and it has also  increased my respect for  people who believe other things because  we need each other remember Carl Bart's  invitation to have a Bible in one hand  and the newspaper in the other and to  interpret news headlines in light of the  Bible and so we remember that inside  these troubling news stories are human  beings treasured coins in the hands of  God that when everything around us seems  like it's falling apart God the Good  Shepherd seeks us out and brings us home  God transform us the saintly and not so  saintly parts of our lives lead us into  repentance lead us into freedom thank  you for seeking us and rejoicing when  what was lost is now found amen
 

